1
00:00:00,359 --> 00:00:03,159
Hi  and  welcome  back  to  Act.

2
00:00:03,159 --> 00:00:04,839
Imagine  you  finished  a  project

3
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:07,720
plan  and  every  box  is  filled  in.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,040
Does  that  mean  the  plan  is  ready?

5
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,360
Not  always.  A  plan  can  look

6
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,160
complete  and  still  depend  on

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,440
assumptions  that  are  too

8
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,439
optimistic,  too  expensive  or  simply

9
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:19,760
wrong.

10
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,119
This  is  why  planning  also  includes

11
00:00:22,119 --> 00:00:23,400
review.

12
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,640
Before  action  starts,  it  is  useful

13
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:27,400
to  look  at  the  plan  again  and

14
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,759
ask  what  could  make  this  difficult

15
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:31,041
in real  life.

16
00:00:31,321 --> 00:00:33,000
Start  by  checking  the  parts  that

17
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,881
depend  on  assumptions.

18
00:00:34,881 --> 00:00:37,161
Maybe  you  expect  100  people  to

19
00:00:37,161 --> 00:00:38,201
attend.

20
00:00:38,201 --> 00:00:39,680
Maybe  you  assume  a  room  is

21
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:40,881
available.

22
00:00:40,881 --> 00:00:42,401
Maybe  you  believe  a  task  will

23
00:00:42,401 --> 00:00:43,921
take  one  day.

24
00:00:43,921 --> 00:00:45,321
Maybe  you  expect  a  certain

25
00:00:45,321 --> 00:00:47,361
resource  to  be  free.

26
00:00:47,361 --> 00:00:48,721
None  of  those  assumptions  are

27
00:00:48,721 --> 00:00:50,641
automatically  bad.

28
00:00:50,641 --> 00:00:52,201
The  problem  is  treating  them  like

29
00:00:52,201 --> 00:00:54,761
facts  before  checking  them.

30
00:00:54,761 --> 00:00:56,721
A  stronger  plan  separates  what  you

31
00:00:56,721 --> 00:00:58,361
know  from  what  you  are  only

32
00:00:58,361 --> 00:00:59,291
expecting.

33
00:00:59,291 --> 00:01:01,411
For  example,  imagine  the  plan

34
00:01:01,411 --> 00:01:03,691
expects  100  participants,  but  the

35
00:01:03,691 --> 00:01:06,331
available  room  only  holds  40.  The

36
00:01:06,331 --> 00:01:08,211
goal  may  still  be  useful,  but  the

37
00:01:08,211 --> 00:01:10,451
plan  has  a  mismatch.  You  could

38
00:01:10,451 --> 00:01:12,411
reduce  the  number,  use  more  than

39
00:01:12,411 --> 00:01:13,930
one  session,  or  look  for  a

40
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:15,371
different  location.

41
00:01:15,371 --> 00:01:17,091
The  important  skill  is  not

42
00:01:17,091 --> 00:01:18,570
protecting  the  first  version  of

43
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:20,490
the  plan.  It  is  noticing  the

44
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:22,611
mismatch  and  adjusting  the  plan

45
00:01:22,611 --> 00:01:24,499
while  the  change  is  still  easy.

46
00:01:24,779 --> 00:01:26,939
The  same  applies  to  resources  and

47
00:01:26,939 --> 00:01:29,459
cost.  Suppose  your  activity  depends

48
00:01:29,459 --> 00:01:31,499
on  equipment  that  costs  twice  the

49
00:01:31,499 --> 00:01:33,939
amount  you  can  realistically  spend.

50
00:01:33,939 --> 00:01:36,098
You  now  have  a  decision  to  make.

51
00:01:36,098 --> 00:01:38,539
Can  you  borrow  it,  replace  it,

52
00:01:38,539 --> 00:01:40,579
reduce  the  scope,  change  the

53
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:42,139
activity?

54
00:01:42,139 --> 00:01:44,499
A  weak  plan  ignores  the  problem

55
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:46,459
and  hopes  it  disappears.

56
00:01:46,459 --> 00:01:48,379
A  stronger  plan  looks  for  an

57
00:01:48,379 --> 00:01:50,018
option  that  still  serves  the

58
00:01:50,018 --> 00:01:52,659
original  need  without  pretending

59
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:54,374
the  limitation  is  not  there.

60
00:01:54,374 --> 00:01:56,414
Time  needs  the  same  kind  of

61
00:01:56,414 --> 00:01:57,414
review.

62
00:01:57,414 --> 00:01:58,974
Ask  whether  tasks  are  in  a

63
00:01:58,974 --> 00:02:01,414
logical  order  and  whether  one  task

64
00:02:01,414 --> 00:02:03,053
depends  on  another.

65
00:02:03,053 --> 00:02:05,174
You  cannot  print  materials  before

66
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:07,294
the  content  is  ready.  You  cannot

67
00:02:07,294 --> 00:02:09,013
confirm  participants  before  the

68
00:02:09,013 --> 00:02:11,054
time  and  place  are  clear.

69
00:02:11,054 --> 00:02:12,733
When  a  task  depends  on  something

70
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:15,254
else,  that  dependency  belongs  in

71
00:02:15,254 --> 00:02:16,454
the  plan.

72
00:02:16,454 --> 00:02:18,653
A  realistic  timeline  is  not  only

73
00:02:18,653 --> 00:02:20,973
a  list  of  dates.  It  reflects  the

74
00:02:20,973 --> 00:02:22,733
order  in  which  the  work  actually

75
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:23,749
has  to  happen.

76
00:02:23,989 --> 00:02:26,428
Practice  can  also  expose  weaknesses

77
00:02:26,428 --> 00:02:28,829
that  planning  alone  may  miss.  When

78
00:02:28,829 --> 00:02:30,789
people  repeat  a  task,  they  often

79
00:02:30,789 --> 00:02:33,468
become  faster,  notice  patterns  and

80
00:02:33,468 --> 00:02:35,109
understand  what  they  need  more

81
00:02:35,109 --> 00:02:36,308
clearly.

82
00:02:36,308 --> 00:02:38,468
The  useful  lesson  is  simple.

83
00:02:38,468 --> 00:02:40,189
Before  an  important  action,  a

84
00:02:40,189 --> 00:02:42,429
small  test  or  rehearsal  can  reveal

85
00:02:42,429 --> 00:02:45,069
missing  steps,  unclear  instructions

86
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:47,165
or  skills  that  still  need  work.

87
00:02:47,645 --> 00:02:50,405
Try  a  short  individual  check.

88
00:02:50,405 --> 00:02:52,405
Imagine  a  community  activity  with

89
00:02:52,405 --> 00:02:55,485
these  details.  50  participants,  one

90
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:57,525
small  room,  no  confirmed

91
00:02:57,525 --> 00:03:00,205
permission,  a  very  limited  budget

92
00:03:00,205 --> 00:03:02,044
and  all  preparation  planned  for

93
00:03:02,044 --> 00:03:03,805
the  final  day.

94
00:03:03,805 --> 00:03:05,604
What  would  you  question  first?

95
00:03:05,604 --> 00:03:07,325
Which  part  needs  evidence,  a

96
00:03:07,325 --> 00:03:09,925
different  option  or  more  time?  You

97
00:03:09,925 --> 00:03:12,364
do  not  need  a  perfect  answer.  The

98
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:13,885
point  is  to  practice  looking  at  a

99
00:03:13,885 --> 00:03:15,245
plan  as  something  that  can  be

100
00:03:15,245 --> 00:03:17,204
tested,  not  something  that  must

101
00:03:17,204 --> 00:03:18,373
must  be  defended.

